            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, it was difficult to find specific locations to address air leakage, but further analysis of the numbers indicate that there are opportunities to save. I suggest air sealing in the following locations:



** Around the attic hatch (can seal with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack")



** At the penetration in the crawl space for the utilidor



** At baseboards, and around window and door frames



Because of the age of your home, I suspect that there is no air barrier within the exterior wall assembly. I have included information later in this report about wall insulation and wall assemblies. 



Savings: A 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss is estimated to save $345 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 8.7 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings>

The attic space of your home appears to have a total of R26 of insulation, comprised of approximately 3.5 inches of vermiculite insulation and R20 fibreglass batts on top. I have modeled the energy savings if you were to increase the attic insulation up to a minimum R50. Please be sure you maintain adequate attic ventilation and do not block any soffit venting.



As there is vermiculite in your attic please read through the warnings and information provided in this report. 



Savings: By increasing all of the attic insulation from R26 up to a minimum R50, this is estimated to save $160 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.1 GJ, and qualify for a $125 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</ceilings>
                <windows>

All of the windows in this house (10 total) are older triple pane wood windows, that do not appear to have  low E coatings. As they all appear to be in good physical shape, and do not appear to be leaky, I do not recommend replacing them at this time. 



However, should you need to replace the windows, I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $170 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 4.3 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</windows>
                <doors>

Your front door is an older solid wood door. I recommend you upgrade this door to an ENERGY STAR?????? rated insulated doors for our climate zone.



Rebates: By replacing this older door with one that is ENERGY STAR?????? qualified for our climate zone, you could potentially save $110 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 2.9 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 184 liter electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. If you are upgrading your boiler, it would be ideal to use your boiler to heat your hot water via a dedicated loop, and incorporate an insulated storage tank to prevent the boiler from "short cycling".



Savings: By using your boiler to heat your hot water, you could potentially save $2,180 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 17.2 GJ. 

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 83% efficient gas boiler. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing gas boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE and using your boiler to heat your domestic hot water instead of your current electric hot water tank.



You currently have a 184 liter electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. If you install a more efficient gas boiler, it would be ideal to use your boiler to heat your hot water via a dedicated loop, and incorporate an insulated storage tank to prevent the boiler from "short cycling".



Savings: By replacing your current gas boiler with a 95% AFUE condensing gas boiler and using it to heat your hot water in addition to space heating, you could potentially save $3,430 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 59.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). While updating your boiler, you should also install ECM circulation pumps and qualify for up to $175 in rebates per pump (up to 2 per fiscal year) from EEIP. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has two older 13 liter toilets that should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush!



Savings: By replacing a toilet with a new 4.8 liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

I have modeled the estimated energy savings if you were to use your current wood stove for about 50% of the heating requirement for your house, compared to the costs of your current gas boiler. 



Rebates &amp; Savings: By using an 80% efficient wood stove for 50% of the heating requirements of the main living area of your house, you could potentially save $1,250 per year in energy costs.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Inuvik, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $400 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Pass

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.1 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace and your wood stove vent up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting.

During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - two bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Inuvik

The following October 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Inuvik:



Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.32 per kWh

Natural Gas - $37.21 per GJ

Oil - $1.68 per liter 

Propane - $1.52 per liter delivered

Wood - $472.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $12.60 per bag, $630.00 per tonne bulk delivered



